To enhance the error handling, you can modify the `handleError` function in `errorHandler.js`. Here's how you might implement it:

```javascript
function handleError(error) {
    console.error(`Error occurred: ${error.message}`);
    if (error.code) {
        console.error(`Error code: ${error.code}`);
    }
    // Add more detailed logging here if necessary
}
```
With this, error messages will now include both the message and code if available, providing better clarity for debugging.

Don't forget to test this functionality after making the changes!

It sounds like the test case in `tests/errorHandler.test.js` may not match the new error format. You'll want to update the test assertions to reflect the changes. Here's an example:

Update the test case:

```javascript
it('should log the correct error format', () => {
    const consoleSpy = jest.spyOn(console, 'error');
    const error = { message: 'Deployment failed', code: 'DEP001' };
    handleError(error);
    expect(consoleSpy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.stringContaining('Error occurred: Deployment failed'));
    expect(consoleSpy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.stringContaining('Error code: DEP001'));
    consoleSpy.mockRestore();
});
```
This change ensures that your test checks for the new structure of the error messages, allowing it to pass.
